---
import Faq from "../components/Faq.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import RelatedLinks from "../components/RelatedLinks.astro";
import { breadcrumbLd, faqLd } from "../lib/schema";

const faqs = [
	{
		q: "What wind speed is safe for a drone?",
		a: "There is no single number. Use the published limit for that model, then stay well under it. On this site, caution starts around 65% of the limit and no go around 85%, including gusts at 10 m, 80 m, and 120 m.",
	},
	{
		q: "What is Level 5 wind resistance?",
		a: "On DJI’s scale, Level 5 is roughly 10.7 to 12 m/s sustained (about 24 to 27 mph). It is a manufacturer class, not a guarantee at 400 ft. See the levels guide for Level 4 and Level 6.",
	},
	{
		q: "Is wind speed the same as gust speed?",
		a: "No. Sustained wind is the average. Gusts are short spikes. Ratings are usually sustained. A gust at the rating is still a problem for a light airframe.",
	},
	{
		q: "How does altitude affect drone wind?",
		a: "Wind often increases with height. 120 m can be several meters per second above what you feel at takeoff. That is why a ground reading alone is a poor go / no go.",
	},
	{
		q: "Does advertised wind resistance guarantee a safe flight?",
		a: "No. It is a spec sheet ceiling in a test condition. Terrain, rotors, rain, and battery all sit outside that number. You still decide.",
	},
];
---

<Layout
	title="Drone wind resistance | what the rating actually means"
	description="Drone wind resistance is a published max sustained wind, not a 400 ft guarantee. Level 5, gusts vs average, and how to use the number before you fly."
	jsonLd={[
		breadcrumbLd([
			{ name: "Home", href: "/" },
			{ name: "Drone wind resistance", href: "/drone-wind-resistance" },
		]),
		faqLd(faqs),
	]}
>
	<Header />
	<main class="page-wrap max-w-3xl py-16">
		<p class="font-mono text-caption uppercase tracking-wide text-mute">Guide</p>
		<h1 class="mt-3 text-display-lg font-semibold text-ink">Drone wind resistance, in plain English.</h1>
		<p class="mt-5 text-body-lg text-body">
			The number on the spec sheet is the strongest sustained wind the manufacturer will still
			call flyable. It is not a dare, and it is not measured at 400 ft AGL.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4 text-body">
			DJI usually quotes a Beaufort style level plus m/s.{" "}
			<a href="/drone-wind-resistance-levels" class="text-link">Level 5</a> is about 10.7 to 12
			m/s. Level 4 is closer to 8 m/s, typical of a{" "}
			<a href="/dji-neo-wind-resistance-calculator" class="text-link">DJI Neo</a>. Gusts can
			blow through that number while the 10 m average still looks fine.
		</p>

		<h2 class="mt-12 text-display-sm font-semibold text-ink">Sustained vs gusts</h2>
		<p class="mt-3 text-body">
			Sustained wind is what the rating describes. Gusts are the kicks. If you only watch a
			single “wind speed” widget, you are often looking at the mean. A Mini class quad cares
			about the kick. This site takes the worse of the two at each height.
		</p>

		<h2 class="mt-12 text-display-sm font-semibold text-ink">Why 10 m is not 400 ft</h2>
		<p class="mt-3 text-body">
			Phone weather and many airports report near 10 m. US recreational ceiling is about 120 m.
			The same front can be a breeze at your knees and over the rating at the top of the
			envelope. That is the gap the{" "}
			<a href="/#calculator" class="text-link">field check</a> is built to show: 10 m, 80 m,
			and 120 m.
		</p>

		<h2 class="mt-12 text-display-sm font-semibold text-ink">How windy is too windy</h2>
		<p class="mt-3 text-body">
			Too windy is when peak wind or gusts eat the buffer on <em>your</em> airframe, not when a
			forum says “15 mph.” A{" "}
			<a href="/dji-mini-4-pro-wind-resistance-calculator" class="text-link">Mini 4 Pro</a> and
			an <a href="/dji-air-3s-wind-resistance-calculator" class="text-link">Air 3S</a> do not
			share a limit.{" "}
			<a href="/how-it-works" class="text-link">How the call works</a> uses about 65% for
			caution and 85% for no go. Those are planning thresholds, not law.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4 text-body">
			Trees, flags, and the sound of the props still beat an app. If they disagree, stay down.
		</p>

		<p class="mt-8">
			<a href="/#calculator" class="font-medium text-link">Check wind for your drone →</a>
		</p>
		<Faq items={faqs} />
		<RelatedLinks
			links={[
				{ href: "/drone-wind-resistance-levels", label: "Wind resistance levels" },
				{ href: "/drone-weather", label: "Drone weather and flight planning" },
				{ href: "/how-it-works", label: "How the verdict is calculated" },
				{ href: "/models", label: "Every model we score" },
			]}
		/>
	</main>
	<Footer />
</Layout>
